#!/bin/sh
# Refuse a commit made on `main` in the MAIN checkout by anyone but the orchestrator.
#
# Runners must commit freely on their own branches in their own worktrees — that is the job —
# so this deliberately narrows to the one case that hurts: a commit landing on the integration
# branch in the shared checkout, which moves `main` under a merge sequence that assumes a single
# writer. Two runners have now done it, and on the second occasion the commit swept up an edit
# the orchestrator was still in the middle of making.
#
# Worktrees share this hooks directory, so the guard must distinguish them. It does that by the
# git dir: the main checkout reports `<repo>/.git`, a worktree reports `<repo>/.git/worktrees/<name>`.
#
# THE GIT DIR IS READ FROM GIT, NOT FROM THE CWD, and that distinction is the whole guard.
# Measured 2026-08-26 (G9); `planning/hooks/prove-pre-commit.sh` re-runs all of it.
#
#   Git runs this hook standing in the WORK TREE, and GIT_WORK_TREE moves the work tree without
#   moving the commit. Committing from the main checkout with `GIT_WORK_TREE` aimed at any linked
#   worktree put the hook's cwd inside that worktree while the commit still landed on `main` in
#   the shared checkout — the exact thing this refuses. A cwd-derived `git rev-parse --git-dir`
#   read `.git/worktrees/<name>`, called it a runner, and waved it through.
#
#   `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir` answers from the environment git set up for the commit, so
#   it names the repository the commit is GOING TO in all three cases: unset GIT_DIR in the main
#   checkout, an exported one inside a worktree, and a redirected work tree. The branch is then
#   read through `--git-dir` for the same reason.
#
# GIT_DIR and GIT_WORK_TREE are unset immediately afterwards. Nothing below reads them, and
# nothing this hook is later asked to invoke should inherit them: git exports GIT_DIR for every
# commit inside a linked worktree, which is every runner commit in this fleet, so a descendant
# that walks into another directory and runs git would operate on the runner's repository rather
# than the one it is standing in. That is DEF-253's mechanism, and one unset closes it for every
# descendant, whatever they turn out to be. It is preferred to an inventory of offending scripts
# because it holds for the scripts nobody has written yet.
#
# The unset does NOT close the GIT_WORK_TREE bypass above — that hole is carried by the cwd, not
# by the variable, and only the git-dir read closes it. Two separate faults, two separate lines.

[ "$MCPR_ORCHESTRATOR" = "1" ] && exit 0

git_dir=$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir) || exit 0

unset GIT_DIR GIT_WORK_TREE

case "$git_dir" in
    */.git/worktrees/*) exit 0 ;;     # a runner in its own worktree: this is its job
esac

branch=$(git --git-dir="$git_dir" symbolic-ref --quiet --short HEAD 2>/dev/null) || exit 0  # detached: not main
[ "$branch" = "main" ] || exit 0

cat >&2 <<'EOF'

  commit refused — this is `main` in the shared checkout, and it is not yours to move.

  Your work belongs on your own branch in your own worktree (.worktrees/<ID>), where
  committing is unrestricted and expected. A commit here moves the integration branch
  under a merge sequence that assumes one writer, and can sweep up an edit someone else
  is still making.

  If something in the main checkout genuinely needs changing, that is a finding for your
  report, not a step to work around.

EOF
exit 1
